10.1177/2053168016652420,216578f767969de4,1,,African American Power and Politics: The Political Context Variable,"Walton H, Jr.",1997,full_text,,correct,argon,"{""target_index"": 1, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""verdict"": ""shipped_right"", ""corrected_value"": null, ""quote"": ""Like Walton (1997: Table 4.3), we trace racial discourse over time and across journals. Figure 2 contains separate scatterplots for each journal."", ""location"": ""Analysis and discussion (lines 181–183, 208–210, 234, 269) and Conclusion (lines 326–333)"", ""reason"": ""Walton (1997, Chapter 4) is a companion book chapter by the first author covering the same PSQ/APSR research program as Walton et al. (1995) and is cited only for conceptual background and as an analytical parallel ('Like Walton (1997: Table 4.3), we trace racial discourse over time and across journals'). Both Figure 1 and Figure 2 explicitly identify the 54-article corpus as the 'Walton et al. Replication Dataset, 1894–1990 (N = 54)' from Walton et al. (1995: Appendix 1), and the paper neither states a distinct empirical finding from Walton (1997) nor reports a separate replication verdict for it. Omitting a second row for Walton (1997) is therefore not an error.""}","{""authors"": ""Walton H, Jr."", ""year"": ""1997"", ""title"": ""African American Power and Politics: The Political Context Variable"", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Like Walton (1997:\nTable 4.3), we trace racial discourse over time and across\njournals."", ""outcome"": """", ""outcome_quote"": """", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""introduction"", ""outcome_computation"": ""not checked"", ""outcome_computational_quote"": ""By building an instrument that gets at\nwhat Walton and his colleagues observe, we re-analyze\ntheir corpus of text in novel ways and apply our instrument\nto current articles in top-tier political science journals."", ""outcome_robustness"": ""robust"", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": ""This\nreplication-and-extension confirms not only that ours and\nWalton’s operationalizations are empirically comparable\nbut also that the former can serve as a proxy for the\nlatter when reviewing the race literature."", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The original 54-publication corpus is re-analyzed using an alternative automated measure of centrality, rather than rerunning the original numerical analysis. The authors explicitly conclude the operationalizations are comparable and the original findings hold up to re-examination. New journal articles in the extension are not treated as robustness tests of original data.""}"
