10.3758/s13428-023-02329-5,49c906aa1fa610be,1,10.1037/xge0000037,When goals conflict with values: Counterproductive attentional and oculomotor capture by reward-related stimuli.,"Le Pelley, M. E., Pearson, D., Griffiths, O., & Beesley, T.",2015,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Le Pelley, M. E., Pearson, D., Griffiths, O., & Beesley, T."", ""year"": ""2015"", ""title"": ""When goals conflict with values: Counterproductive attentional and oculomotor capture by reward-related stimuli."", ""doi"": ""10.1037/xge0000037"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""The first aim of the present study was to replicate and extend the results of Watson et al. (\n2019a\n), which demonstrated that the VMAC effect could be observed in conditions where a high reward predicting singleton had always been response irrelevant and held no informational value. In our study, we showed that the VMAC effect increased over time in the rewarded phase, that is, we observed a progressive increase in RTs when the high valued singleton acted as singleton distractor compared to the low valued singleton. Furthermore, when reward feedback was omitted in the unrewarded phase, the VMAC effect remained constant, without any evidence of reduction over trials. These two results replicate previous findings with Le Pelley’s paradigm (Le Pelley et al.,\n2015\n), where the VMAC effect emerged over trials, even when the reward-signals are always task irrelevant, and also replicate Watson et al. (\n2019a\n), who showed that once established, the VMAC effect remains present regardless of the current informational value of the reward-related stimuli."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""The first aim of the present study was to replicate and extend the results of Watson et al. (\n2019a\n), which demonstrated that the VMAC effect could be observed in conditions where a high reward predicting singleton had always been response irrelevant and held no informational value. In our study, we showed that the VMAC effect increased over time in the rewarded phase, that is, we observed a progressive increase in RTs when the high valued singleton acted as singleton distractor compared to the low valued singleton. Furthermore, when reward feedback was omitted in the unrewarded phase, the VMAC effect remained constant, without any evidence of reduction over trials. These two results replicate previous findings with Le Pelley’s paradigm (Le Pelley et al.,\n2015\n), where the VMAC effect emerged over trials, even when the reward-signals are always task irrelevant, and also replicate Watson et al. (\n2019a\n), who showed that once established, the VMAC effect remains present regardless of the current informational value of the reward-related stimuli."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The authors explicitly state that their new-sample results replicate this finding.""}"
10.1177/20531680231205157,6d33998f7d1c5c95,1,10.1257/aer.20191435,The welfare effects of social media,"Allcott H, Braghieri L, Eichmeyer S, et al.",2020,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Allcott H, Braghieri L, Eichmeyer S, et al."", ""year"": ""2020"", ""title"": ""The welfare effects of social media"", ""doi"": ""10.1257/aer.20191435"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""We also find support for recent findings (Allcott et al., 2020; Asimovic et al., 2021) that Facebook deactivation leads to a reduction in anxiety levels and suggestive evidence of a reduction in knowledge of current news, though the latter is again limited to our 2021 subsample."", ""outcome"": ""mixed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""We also find support for recent findings (Allcott et al., 2020; Asimovic et al., 2021) that Facebook deactivation leads to a reduction in anxiety levels and suggestive evidence of a reduction in knowledge of current news, though the latter is again limited to our 2021 subsample."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""abstract"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Anxiety agrees, but news knowledge agrees only in 2021; the pooled and 2020 news effects are absent. These secondary findings are explicitly preregistered replication targets.""}"
10.1080/02687038.2010.536841,1ef8938bc28953a5,0,10.1177/0956797611404897,The use of definite references signals declarative memory: Evidence from hippocampal amnesia,"Duff MC, Gupta R, Hengst J, Tranel D, Cohen NJ",in press,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Duff MC, Gupta R, Hengst J, Tranel D, Cohen NJ"", ""year"": ""in press"", ""title"": ""The use of definite references signals declarative memory: Evidence from hippocampal amnesia"", ""doi"": ""10.1177/0956797611404897"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Of particular interest was how similar the use of definite initiating reference in the present study, when the familiar partners of the amnesia participants were the directors, when compared with our previous work ( Duff et al., in press ), when the amnesia participants were the directors."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""The data displayed that the difference between amnesia and comparison pairs in the use of indefinite references observed in Duff et al. (in press) was not limited to the productions of the amnesia participants but also extended to their partners without brain damage."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Authors explicitly extend the original observed difference to communication partners and compare the role-switched new observations to their previous results.""}"
