10.1080/22243534.2015.11828344,1c44b19bafd0d2c9,0,,,Hofstede,,abstract,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Hofstede"", ""year"": """", ""title"": """", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": """", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""In the present study the cultural dimensions of the well known Dutch researcher Geert Hofstede were tested on a sample of international business students."", ""outcome"": ""mixed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""The findings of the present research contest many of Hofstede's original findings. The dimensions power distance and long-term orientation show significant differences with Hofstede's original country values. The dimensions individualism, masculinity and uncertainty avoidance show significant differences for half of the countries."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""abstract"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": false, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Abstract contests many original findings, with discrepancies on two dimensions and only half the countries on three dimensions; coded as partial agreement/disagreement with uncertainty because no full results are available. No reference list supplied, so cannot resolve whether the underlying original findings belong to one or multiple Hofstede publications.""}"
10.1111/jcal.12211,73bcc3e73f111e2c,0,10.1016/j.compedu.2017.05.013,Seeing the instructor's face and gaze in demonstration video examples affects attention allocation but not learning.,"Van Wermeskerken, M., & Van Gog, T.",2017,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Van Wermeskerken, M., & Van Gog, T."", ""year"": ""2017"", ""title"": ""Seeing the instructor's face and gaze in demonstration video examples affects attention allocation but not learning."", ""doi"": ""10.1016/j.compedu.2017.05.013"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""This study replicated the face visible and face not visible conditions from our prior study (Van Wermeskerken & Van Gog, 2017) with adolescents in prevocational education, using the same molecule building task, to investigate whether or not seeing the model's face would affect learning outcomes in a younger population with a lower level of education."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""With respect to the first aim, we replicated our previous findings in this adolescent population, by showing that their learning was not affected by the presence or absence of the model's face, even though they spent proportionally less time looking at the task area when the model's face was visible."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Authors explicitly state replication of their prior findings; the earlier Van Gog et al. paper is background in the replication chain, not the closest target.""}"
