10.2307/2586310,b91a64486d8bd684,1,,,"MacKuen, Erikson, and Stimson",1992,abstract,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""MacKuen, Erikson, and Stimson"", ""year"": ""1992"", ""title"": """", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""MacKuen, Erikson, and Stimson (1989, 1992) argue that the aggregate distribution of party identification, or macropartisanship, shifts significantly over short periods in response to changes in presidential popularity and consumer sentiment. We replicate this analysis using a more extensive inventory of Gallup and CBS/New York Timesdata and find considerably less evidence of partisan fluctuation."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""The amount of partisan change caused by short-term movements in consumer sentiment and presidential popularity is found to be two to three times smaller than initially reported by MacKuen et al. (1989). Our results indicate that macropartisanship adjusts to short-term shocks in a limited and gradual fashion, consistent with traditional views of partisan realignment."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""abstract"", ""outcome_confident"": false, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The abstract describes both cited originals as reporting responsiveness to these inputs and reports responsiveness in the same direction but smaller. Reduced magnitude alone does not imply mixed. Full-text verdict and reference entries unavailable.""}"
10.1080/0003684042000229532,236d200a82c3a67f,1,,"Career mobility, job match, and overeducation",Robst J.,1995,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Robst J."", ""year"": ""1995"", ""title"": ""Career mobility, job match, and overeducation"", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""In Section 4.3, we extend Robst’s (1995) test for robustness by using an objective, as well as a subjective, measure of training participation."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Both measures confirm Robst’s findings that overeducated (undereducated) workers indeed receive less (more) training than correctly allocated ones."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""introduction"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true}"
10.1016/j.learninstruc.2018.10.002,f62411717f6082b5,0,,,,2008,abstract,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": """", ""year"": ""2008"", ""title"": """", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""3"", ""evidence_quote"": ""Lastly, in Experiment 3, we attempted to replicate the work of Palm (2008) who was able to triple the number of realistic responses by creating problems with a richer backstory and providing more detail. Our sample of sixth-grade students failed to show an increase in realistic responses to these, enhanced, versions of realistic word problems."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Lastly, in Experiment 3, we attempted to replicate the work of Palm (2008) who was able to triple the number of realistic responses by creating problems with a richer backstory and providing more detail. Our sample of sixth-grade students failed to show an increase in realistic responses to these, enhanced, versions of realistic word problems."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""abstract"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Abstract explicitly reports an unsuccessful replication of Palm (2008). The reference list is unavailable, so citation fields beyond the printed year cannot be recovered.""}"
10.1080/10573569.2020.1860841,5e1e14843d69c7cd,0,,,,,abstract,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": """", ""year"": """", ""title"": """", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": """", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""This study conducted a conceptual replication examining the reliability, stability, and sensitivity to growth of slopes produced from CBM-W."", ""outcome"": ""cannot_be_determined"", ""outcome_quote"": """", ""outcome_quote_section"": """", ""outcome_confident"": false, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Abstract calls this a conceptual replication but does not name the original paper or state its findings. Negative results alone cannot establish disagreement with an unstated original finding; target identity and count remain unresolved.""}"
10.5964/jnc.7029,7113daab150388e1,0,10.1016/0010-0277(90)90003-3,Children's understanding of counting,"Wynn, K.",1990,full_text,,error,argon,"{""target_index"": 0, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""verdict"": ""blind_right"", ""corrected_value"": {""classification"": ""omitted target"", ""doi"": ""10.1016/0010-0277(90)90003-3"", ""title"": ""Children's understanding of counting"", ""authors"": ""Wynn, K."", ""year"": ""1990"", ""type"": ""replication"", ""outcome"": ""mixed""}, ""quote"": ""The present work focused on the GaN from Wynn's (1990Wynn's ( , 1992) ) studies with no intention to test the replicability of results coming from the other tasks (i.e., the Point-to-x task or the colour control task were not administered)."", ""location"": ""Study 1, Materials (line 17)"", ""reason"": ""Omitted target. The authors explicitly frame their work as a conceptual replication of the Give-a-Number (GaN) observations from both Wynn (1990) and Wynn (1992), and in Study 1 they directly re-test Wynn's (1990) counter vs. grabber strategy predictions (line 30) as well as the all-at-once generalization assumption attributed to Wynn (1990, 1992) (line 28), finding mixed support (confirming that large-number knowers count more frequently for large than small numbers, but finding sequential acquisition of large number words and no difference between terminal-counters and terminal-grabbers in reaching large-number knowledge).""}","{""authors"": ""Wynn, K."", ""year"": ""1990"", ""title"": ""Children's understanding of counting"", ""doi"": ""10.1016/0010-0277(90)90003-3"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""1"", ""evidence_quote"": ""The present work focused on the GaN from Wynn's (1990Wynn's ( , 1992) ) studies with no intention to test the replicability of results coming from the other tasks (i.e., the Point-to-x task or the colour control task were not administered)."", ""outcome"": ""mixed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Supporting this view, Large-numbers knowers (4-to 7-knowers) were found to be more likely to resort to counting for large number words than for small ones in study 1. In addition, several children switched from a grabbing to a counting strategy around four. However, the majority of participants kept a consistent strategy all along the task and many of them still succeeded at giving large numbers with a grabbing strategy. Thus, the strategy, at least as coded here, could not be considered as what makes the difference to acquire the cardinal knowledge of large number words."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The authors explicitly report agreement with some original observations and disagreement with others; no overall replication verdict supersedes those comparisons.""}"
10.1353/csd.2014.0018,7f3121c09ebf8efd,0,,,,1979,abstract,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": """", ""year"": ""1979"", ""title"": """", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": """", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Findings from a 1979 study of career services offices (CSOs) were compared to the current findings."", ""outcome"": ""cannot_be_determined"", ""outcome_quote"": """", ""outcome_quote_section"": """", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": false, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The abstract states that findings were compared, but neither identifies the original citation nor states its findings or the replication comparison outcome.""}"
10.1002/hbm.25386,542be9dcc424519c,0,10.1001/jamapsychiatry.2018.2467,Mapping the heterogeneous phenotype of schizophrenia and bipolar disorder using normative models.,"Wolfers, T., Doan, N. T., Kaufmann, T., Alnæs, D., Moberget, T., Agartz, I., … Marquand, A. F.",2018,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Wolfers, T., Doan, N. T., Kaufmann, T., Alnæs, D., Moberget, T., Agartz, I., … Marquand, A. F."", ""year"": ""2018"", ""title"": ""Mapping the heterogeneous phenotype of schizophrenia and bipolar disorder using normative models."", ""doi"": ""10.1001/jamapsychiatry.2018.2467"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""1, 2"", ""evidence_quote"": ""Due to these important implications, we here report an attempt to replicate and extend our initial findings in two independent samples acquired on different scanners following an identical analytical procedure as in our previous discovery study."", ""outcome"": ""mixed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Our replications support that group level‐differences in brain structure disguise considerable individual differences in brain aberrations. While we find additional similarity across discovery and replication study (Figures S2 and S3), such as extreme negative deviations are on average 3.91, 4.00, and 4.95 times more prevalent in individuals with schizophrenia than in healthy controls, we also find differences. Especially with respect to extreme positive deviations the pattern of overlap is as similar as it is different across studies (Figure S4)."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Authors affirm the heterogeneity finding but explicitly describe both similarities and differences, including failure of the bipolar cerebellar group effect. The conclusion supporting the main heterogeneity claim concerns one effect, rather than all tested comparisons. Additional contrast: Further, we could not replicate a main group effect of bipolar disorder in comparison with healthy individuals in the cerebellum while this effect was present in the discovery sample (Figure 2).""}"
10.1080/15475441.2014.979387,fa44ae0be27e328b,1,10.1016/j.cognition.2013.02.011,The acquisition of abstract words by young infants.,Bergelson E; Swingley D,2013b,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Bergelson E; Swingley D"", ""year"": ""2013b"", ""title"": ""The acquisition of abstract words by young infants."", ""doi"": ""10.1016/j.cognition.2013.02.011"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""This study confirms several patterns that have emerged in previous work ( Bergelson & Swingley, 2012 , 2013b ; Parise & Csibra, 2012 ; Tincoff & Jusczyk, 1999 , 2012 ). Namely, we find further evidence, with a new sample of infants and a new set of item pairs, that 6-9 month olds understand words."", ""outcome"": ""mixed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""While the study was not designed to test the difference between noun and verb acquisition, 6–9-month-olds did perform well on one of the two verb pairs ( hug - eat ), contrary to their poor performance on a broader set of non-nouns in a prior study ( Bergelson & Swingley, 2013 ). Whether infants’ apparent success on hug-eat represents random item variation, or a first fragile glimmer of infants’ understanding of these verbs, is unclear, particularly given infants’ failure to demonstrate understanding of kiss-drink here."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The authors explicitly contrast success on one verb pair with prior non-noun failure while another pair fails; they also confirm the age boost from this original. The broad sentence about confirming several patterns does not give an overall verdict on the entire replication of this paper. Agreement passage: Not unexpectedly, this ability increases with age, but perhaps less expectedly the rate with which it increases is non-linear. This is the third study that finds evidence of a boost in performance around 13-14 months, independent of word type ( Bergelson & Swingley, 2012 , 2013b ).""}"
