10.1037/pspp0000104,9c0e3d488066f983,0,,"Religiosity, self-esteem, and psychological adjustment: On the cross-cultural specificity of the benefits of religiosity.","Gebauer, J. E., Sedikides, C., & Neberich, W.",2012,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Gebauer, J. E., Sedikides, C., & Neberich, W."", ""year"": ""2012"", ""title"": ""Religiosity, self-esteem, and psychological adjustment: On the cross-cultural specificity of the benefits of religiosity."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""1, 2, 3"", ""evidence_quote"": ""Study 1 was a direct replication of previous cross-cultural research on the religiosity- adjustment relation. Yet, this study differed from previous research in three important ways. First, previous research operationalized psychological adjustment as life satisfaction (Stavrova et al., 2013), subjective well-being (Diener et al., 2011), depression (Leurent et al., 2013), or social self-esteem (Gebauer et al., 2012b). "", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Our results fully and firmly replicated and extended prior evidence for cross-cultural differences in religious adjustment benefits."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""abstract"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The reported moderation agrees with the prior positive moderation findings.""}"
