10.1177/2053168018822174,6070e18087845e69,4,,Rumors and health care reform: Experiments in political misinformation.,Berinsky AJ,2017,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Berinsky AJ"", ""year"": ""2017"", ""title"": ""Rumors and health care reform: Experiments in political misinformation."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""5"", ""evidence_quote"": ""We attempted to replicate Berinsky’s 2017 experiment on belief in rumors surrounding the Affordable Care Act (ACA), specifically the false rumor that the ACA would create “death panels” that would make end-of-life decisions for patients without their consent."", ""outcome"": ""mixed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Treatment effect heterogeneity for the healthcare rumors is also low for both the original Berinsky (2017) sample and for the Lucid sample (Figure 5). Treatment effects for all possible treatment possibilities are similar for both Democrats and Republicans for both samples. It is important to note that while we do not replicate the original ATEs for this study in the previous section, here we see that the CATEs are statistically differentiable for only Democrats receiving the Democratic correction to the healthcare rumor."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""results"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Direct comparison against identified experimental source; Berinsky average effects differ while heterogeneity agrees.""}"
