10.1097/00005650-199204000-00001,535f4428e6ed49dd,1,,"Relations between physicians’ behaviors and analogue patients’ satisfaction, recall and impressions","Roter, D. L., Hall, J. A., Katz, N. R.",1987,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Roter, D. L., Hall, J. A., Katz, N. R."", ""year"": ""1987"", ""title"": ""Relations between physicians’ behaviors and analogue patients’ satisfaction, recall and impressions"", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""In the last of her two papers about the\nrelative relevance of instrumental or task-re-\nlated behavior on one hand, and socio-emo-\ntional or affective behavior on the other,\nRoter called on other researchers “‘to repli-\ncate her findings through different method-\nologies and in the natural setting.’”* This call\nwas based on two possible weaknesses of\nher study: 1) the use of simulated patients\ninstead of real patients, which raises ques-\ntions about the generalization of the results,\nand 2) the use of audiotapes instead of video-\ntapes, which limited the possibility to regis-\nter nonverbal affect to vocal-tone, only. Our\nstudy can be seen as such a replication: it\ntook place in the natural setting (real general\npractitioners with real patients who had hy-\npertension) and used partly different meth-\nodologies (video instead of audio, which\nmade it possible to register visual as well vo-\ncal cues). In addition, some refinements\nhave been made in RIAS to get a more de-\ntailed picture of GP’s socio-emotional behav-\nior."", ""outcome"": ""mixed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""The results presented partly corroborate\nthose from the previous studies in which\nRoter’s Interaction Analysis System was\nused, but they also shed some new light on\nthe controversies found in the literature\nabout the relative relevance of instrumental\nversus affective behavior."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Discussion explicitly describes partial corroboration of the earlier RIAS studies, then identifies both shared associations and important differences, including failure to corroborate the overall primacy of task-related behavior.""}"
10.1353/hub.2006.0033,6d0a7118936c5d54,0,,,,,abstract,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": """", ""year"": """", ""title"": """", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": """", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Our previous studies showed a statistically significant association between UBL5 sequence variation and several obesity- and diabetes-related quantitative physiological measures in Asian Indian and Micronesian cohorts. Here we undertake a replication study in a Mexican American cohort where the original linkage signal was first detected."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""These data are consistent with our earlier published studies and further support a functional role for the UBL5 gene in influencing physiological traits that underpin the development of metabolic syndrome."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""abstract"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Abstract gives a collective agreement verdict for the previous UBL5 association studies; bibliography is unavailable.""}"
10.2174/1874402800902010006,498d97fc3bf87bf8,0,,A pilot study of functional magnetic resonance imaging brain correlates of deception in healthy young men.,"Kozel, F. A.; Revell, L. J.; Lorberbaum, J. P.; Shastri, A.; Elhai, J. D.; Horner, M. D.; Smith, A.; Nahas, Z.; Bohning, D. E.; George, M. S.",2004,full_text,competing_supported_matches,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Kozel, F. A.; Revell, L. J.; Lorberbaum, J. P.; Shastri, A.; Elhai, J. D.; Horner, M. D.; Smith, A.; Nahas, Z.; Bohning, D. E.; George, M. S."", ""year"": ""2004"", ""title"": ""A pilot study of functional magnetic resonance imaging brain correlates of deception in healthy young men."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""The Lie-minus-True group map revealed significant\nactivation in similar regions from the prior cohorts using this\nparadigm (see Figure and Table). Specifically, the regions of\nright orbitofrontal, right inferior frontal, right middle frontal,\nand right cingulate gyrus have replicated now in five\nindependent groups from four studies across several\nparadigms [3-5]."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""This method for detecting deception was successfully\nreplicated on a different scanner at a different location for\nboth the individual and group analyses."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The explicit overall verdict states successful replication for both group and individual analyses. It covers the group-level findings attributed to references 3–5, outweighing the specific left temporal contrast.""}"
