10.3389/fpsyg.2018.02374,654919670700751a,0,10.1162/jocn.2007.19.11.1872,How negative emotion enhances the visual specificity of a memory.,"Kensinger E. A., Garoff-Eaton R. J., Schacter D. L.",2007,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Kensinger E. A., Garoff-Eaton R. J., Schacter D. L."", ""year"": ""2007"", ""title"": ""How negative emotion enhances the visual specificity of a memory."", ""doi"": ""10.1162/jocn.2007.19.11.1872"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""In this study, we provide a conceptual replication of Kensinger’s findings.\nSpecifically, while positive valence seems to have little effect, negative valence appears to significantly increase the amount of details that are remembered. For example,\nKensinger et al. (2007)\nhave shown that participants are more accurate in remembering visual details of negative valanced items (e.g., “a green and black snake with yellow eyes”) compared to positive items (e.g., “a cake”). Obviously, there could be visual confounds that might lead to more detailed memory for negative compared to positive stimuli. It has been shown, however, that even if visual confounds are controlled for, the effect of valence on memory accuracy prevails. One prominent example is the study by\nKensinger and Schacter (2006)\n, which measured the amount of details Red Sox fans and Yankee fans could report from a baseball game and found that supporters of the losing team had more detailed memories. In this instance, the visual information was matched between the groups, but what differed was the either positive or negative appraisal of the event that led to differences in memory accuracy. "", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""The results obtained from 60 healthy human participants confirmed Kensinger’s hypothesis by showing that under conditions of high visual interference, negative valanced emotions led to higher levels of recognition accuracy compared to neutral and positive emotions."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""abstract"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The overall confirmation verdict covers the behavioral negative-valence detail-memory finding retested with auditory induction.""}"
10.3389/fpsyg.2018.02374,654919670700751a,1,10.3758/BF03193993,When the red sox shocked the yankees: comparing negative and positive memories.,"Kensinger E. A., Schacter D. L.",2006,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Kensinger E. A., Schacter D. L."", ""year"": ""2006"", ""title"": ""When the red sox shocked the yankees: comparing negative and positive memories."", ""doi"": ""10.3758/BF03193993"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""In this study, we provide a conceptual replication of Kensinger’s findings.\nSpecifically, while positive valence seems to have little effect, negative valence appears to significantly increase the amount of details that are remembered. For example,\nKensinger et al. (2007)\nhave shown that participants are more accurate in remembering visual details of negative valanced items (e.g., “a green and black snake with yellow eyes”) compared to positive items (e.g., “a cake”). Obviously, there could be visual confounds that might lead to more detailed memory for negative compared to positive stimuli. It has been shown, however, that even if visual confounds are controlled for, the effect of valence on memory accuracy prevails. One prominent example is the study by\nKensinger and Schacter (2006)\n, which measured the amount of details Red Sox fans and Yankee fans could report from a baseball game and found that supporters of the losing team had more detailed memories. In this instance, the visual information was matched between the groups, but what differed was the either positive or negative appraisal of the event that led to differences in memory accuracy. "", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""The results obtained from 60 healthy human participants confirmed Kensinger’s hypothesis by showing that under conditions of high visual interference, negative valanced emotions led to higher levels of recognition accuracy compared to neutral and positive emotions."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""abstract"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The overall confirmation verdict covers the behavioral negative-valence detail-memory finding retested with auditory induction.""}"
10.1002/pon.4841,b102ffce14deaaa3,1,,Un Abrazo Para La Familia: An evidenced-based rehabilitation approach in providing cancer education to low-SES Hispanic co-survivors,"Marshall, CA; Curran, MA; Koerner, SS; Kroll, T; Hickman, A; García, F",2014,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Marshall, CA; Curran, MA; Koerner, SS; Kroll, T; Hickman, A; García, F"", ""year"": ""2014"", ""title"": ""Un Abrazo Para La Familia: An evidenced-based rehabilitation approach in providing cancer education to low-SES Hispanic co-survivors"", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""description"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""The objective of the Portland replication was to train 20 promotoras to implement Abrazo in their communities with at least 120 Latino cancer survivors and co-survivors."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Portland promotoras demonstrated that when the Abrazo intervention is provided via a culturally congruent, accessible format, the significant gains in cancer knowledge and self-efficacy reported by underserved co-survivors can be replicated."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""abstract"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The authors explicitly state that the Portland implementation replicated the prior Tucson gains in knowledge and self-efficacy.""}"
10.1017/s0261444818000368,259ed3faa8c43fba,1,10.1017/S027226319800103X,"Recasts, repetition, and ambiguity in L2 classroom discourse","Lyster, R.",1998,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Lyster, R."", ""year"": ""1998"", ""title"": ""Recasts, repetition, and ambiguity in L2 classroom discourse"", ""doi"": ""10.1017/S027226319800103X"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Finally, these replication results confirmed the results of prior research that showed a difference in response rates based on the linguistic error targeted in recasts, with recasts for lexical errors eliciting more targetlike responses than recasts targeting grammatical errors (Lyster 1998; Mackey et al. 2000; Carpenter et al. 2006; Loewen & Philp 2006)."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Finally, these replication results confirmed the results of prior research that showed a difference in response rates based on the linguistic error targeted in recasts, with recasts for lexical errors eliciting more targetlike responses than recasts targeting grammatical errors (Lyster 1998; Mackey et al. 2000; Carpenter et al. 2006; Loewen & Philp 2006)."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Explicit group-level comparison states that this study confirms the lexical-versus-grammatical error result attributed to each named original; this was tested in the current regression. DOI reproduced as printed, including any typographical error.""}"
