10.3758/s13414-015-0849-x,ae78ae9e5572ca14,1,10.3758/BF03193553,Asymmetric interference between sex and emotion in face perception.,"Atkinson, A. P., Tipples, J., Burt, D. M., & Young, A. W.",2005,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Atkinson, A. P., Tipples, J., Burt, D. M., & Young, A. W."", ""year"": ""2005"", ""title"": ""Asymmetric interference between sex and emotion in face perception."", ""doi"": ""10.3758/BF03193553"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""1b, 2b"", ""evidence_quote"": ""Experiment\n2b\nwas designed to assess whether the same symmetrical pattern of results would emerge if a smaller set of individuals drawn from the new face set was used. On the basis of the results reported by Le Gal and Bruce (\n2002\n), Atkinson et al. (\n2005\n), Karnadewi and Lipp (\n2011\n), and in Experiment\n1b\n, no symmetrical interaction was expected."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Resembling the pattern seen in Experiment 1, Experiment\n2b\nfailed to replicate the symmetrical interaction between face sex and expression on expression and sex categorization seen for the larger picture set used in Experiment\n2a\n. Rather, an asymmetrical interaction was found, in that poser sex affected the categorization of emotional expressions, whereas expression did not affect the categorization of faces by sex. This pattern deviates from that seen in Experiment\n1b\n, in which no interaction was observed in either sex or expression categorization, but is consistent with the pattern of results reported in studies that had employed the Garner paradigm (Atkinson et al.,\n2005\n; Karnadewi & Lipp,\n2011\n)."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Authors compare the small-set asymmetrical result to these published results as consistent; absence of symmetry is the selected claim tested.""}"
