10.31234/osf.io/prfm3,2480054a833f1b61,0,,,Kleiman and Rule,2013,abstract,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Kleiman and Rule"", ""year"": ""2013"", ""title"": """", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Kleiman and Rule (2013) demonstrated that naive observers could categorize static images at above chance levels according to suicidality and that the faces of individuals who died by suicide were rated as more impulsive than control faces. We attempted to replicate these results with a larger sample and extended the original report by implementing a 2AFC paradigm that minimizes response bias."", ""outcome"": ""mixed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""We found that suicidality categorization was above-chance using the original testing paradigm, but performance dropped to chance in our 2AFC task. We found no evidence supporting differences in perceived impulsivity across categories in either paradigm."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""abstract"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Original paradigm reproduced suicidality classification; the alternative paradigm did not, and the original impulsivity difference was unsupported in both.""}"
10.1016/j.jecp.2015.11.007,7deb1670745402ab,0,,Online usage of theory of mind continues to develop in late adolescence.,"Dumontheil, I., Apperly, I. A., & Blakemore, S. J.",2010,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Dumontheil, I., Apperly, I. A., & Blakemore, S. J."", ""year"": ""2010"", ""title"": ""Online usage of theory of mind continues to develop in late adolescence."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""The current study had three main objectives: (a) to replicate the behavioral findings of Dumontheil and colleagues (2010), (b) to assess the role of inhibitory control in this task across age groups, and (c) to compare the time course of information processing among children, adolescents, and adults through eye-tracking in order to determine in what ways the deployment of ToM differs across age groups."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""To summarize, children and adolescents made more errors than adults only in the Director Experimental trials, which required taking the perspective of the director into account, in contrast to the rule-based control condition (No-Director Experimental trials). This replicated the behavioral results reported in Dumontheil and colleagues (2010)."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""results"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The stated target is the developmental behavioral finding. Accuracy and reaction-time comparisons agree with the original. Other sources motivate theories, tasks or new inhibitory-control/eye-tracking analyses and are not identified as specific replication targets.""}"
