10.23668/psycharchives.4766,3617489aca51758c,12,10.21449/ijate.615640,An English version of the mathematics teaching anxiety scale.,"Hunt, T. E., & Sari, M. H.",2019,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Hunt, T. E., & Sari, M. H."", ""year"": ""2019"", ""title"": ""An English version of the mathematics teaching anxiety scale."", ""doi"": ""10.21449/ijate.615640"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""However, in contrast to previous data (Hunt & Sari, 2019), our results provided no evidence of a beneficial effect of teaching math on the teachers’ attitudes, because female in-service and pre-service elementary school teachers did not significantly differ in enjoyment and ease of teaching math, and experience level in female in-service teachers did not significantly alter their teaching attitudes."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""However, in contrast to previous data (Hunt & Sari, 2019), our results provided no evidence of a beneficial effect of teaching math on the teachers’ attitudes, because female in-service and pre-service elementary school teachers did not significantly differ in enjoyment and ease of teaching math, and experience level in female in-service teachers did not significantly alter their teaching attitudes."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The authors explicitly compare their own result with this cited original finding. The stated agreement or disagreement determines this target’s outcome.""}"
10.31235/osf.io/zugae,ad7125f74e82a532,1,10.4324/9781315696485,The Effect of Firearms on Suicide.,"Kleck, G.",2018,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Kleck, G."", ""year"": ""2018"", ""title"": ""The Effect of Firearms on Suicide."", ""doi"": ""10.4324/9781315696485"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Using a Poisson\nregression with the data in Kleck (2018, Table 17.3), I compared lethality of firearm (258,386 deaths, 308,623\nattempts, 83.7% lethal) and hanging/suffocation suicide attempts (118,072 deaths, 154,013 attempts, 76.7%\nlethal). Firearm suicide attempts are 8.8% more deadly, or conversely, hanging/suffocation attempts are 36.0%\nmore surivavable (both p < 0.001)."", ""outcome"": """", ""outcome_quote"": """", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""fulltext"", ""outcome_computation"": ""not checked"", ""outcome_computational_quote"": ""Using a Poisson\nregression with the data in Kleck (2018, Table 17.3), I compared lethality of firearm (258,386 deaths, 308,623\nattempts, 83.7% lethal) and hanging/suffocation suicide attempts (118,072 deaths, 154,013 attempts, 76.7%\nlethal). Firearm suicide attempts are 8.8% more deadly, or conversely, hanging/suffocation attempts are 36.0%\nmore surivavable (both p < 0.001)."", ""outcome_robustness"": ""robustness challenges"", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": ""Using a Poisson\nregression with the data in Kleck (2018, Table 17.3), I compared lethality of firearm (258,386 deaths, 308,623\nattempts, 83.7% lethal) and hanging/suffocation suicide attempts (118,072 deaths, 154,013 attempts, 76.7%\nlethal). Firearm suicide attempts are 8.8% more deadly, or conversely, hanging/suffocation attempts are 36.0%\nmore surivavable (both p < 0.001)."", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Footnote reanalyses the original published counts with Poisson regression, overturning the stated no-difference interpretation; it does not attempt to rerun original numbers.""}"
10.5964/jnc.6063,d667da1d0d43e70a,1,10.5951/AT.32.5.0051,A study of math anxiety/math avoidance in preservice elementary teachers.,"Kelly, W. P., & Tomhave, W. K.",1985,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Kelly, W. P., & Tomhave, W. K."", ""year"": ""1985"", ""title"": ""A study of math anxiety/math avoidance in preservice elementary teachers."", ""doi"": ""10.5951/AT.32.5.0051"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""The current study replicated the finding that female elementary school teachers have a higher level of math anxiety in relation to female students from other fields (【10†Hembree, 1990】; 【26†Kelly & Tomhave, 1985】; 【32†Szczygieł & Cipora, 2016】)."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""The current study replicated the finding that female elementary school teachers have a higher level of math anxiety in relation to female students from other fields (【10†Hembree, 1990】; 【26†Kelly & Tomhave, 1985】; 【32†Szczygieł & Cipora, 2016】)."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Authors explicitly state that this finding replicated. Specialization is an extension; smaller effects and inconclusive Bayes factors do not override their verdict.""}"
10.5964/jnc.6063,d667da1d0d43e70a,2,10.5604/01.3001.0009.4827,Lęk przed matematyką przyszłych nauczycieli edukacji przedszkolnej i wczesnoszkolnej. Jak uczyć kiedy sama się boję? (Maths anxiety among future pre-service elementary school teachers: How to teach when I am anxious as well?).,"Szczygieł, M., & Cipora, K.",2016,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Szczygieł, M., & Cipora, K."", ""year"": ""2016"", ""title"": ""Lęk przed matematyką przyszłych nauczycieli edukacji przedszkolnej i wczesnoszkolnej. Jak uczyć kiedy sama się boję? (Maths anxiety among future pre-service elementary school teachers: How to teach when I am anxious as well?)."", ""doi"": ""10.5604/01.3001.0009.4827"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""The current study replicated the finding that female elementary school teachers have a higher level of math anxiety in relation to female students from other fields (【10†Hembree, 1990】; 【26†Kelly & Tomhave, 1985】; 【32†Szczygieł & Cipora, 2016】)."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""The current study replicated the finding that female elementary school teachers have a higher level of math anxiety in relation to female students from other fields (【10†Hembree, 1990】; 【26†Kelly & Tomhave, 1985】; 【32†Szczygieł & Cipora, 2016】)."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Authors explicitly state that this finding replicated. Specialization is an extension; smaller effects and inconclusive Bayes factors do not override their verdict.""}"
10.1080/14459795.2022.2135227,cd0616d25cb2d32e,1,10.1098/rsos.200090,A direct and conceptual replication of post-loss speeding when gambling.,"Eben, C., Chen, Z., Vermeylen, L., Billieux, J., & Verbruggen, F.",2020,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Eben, C., Chen, Z., Vermeylen, L., Billieux, J., & Verbruggen, F."", ""year"": ""2020"", ""title"": ""A direct and conceptual replication of post-loss speeding when gambling."", ""doi"": ""10.1098/rsos.200090"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""1, 2"", ""evidence_quote"": ""Across experi-\nments, we could again replicate the finding by Langer and Roth (1975) in an online ex-\nperimental setting (see also our replication study). Thus, introducing a start response\ndid not alter the illusion-of-control effect. Moreover, we replicated the finding that par-\nticipants speed up after losses compared to wins (Eben et al., 2020; Verbruggen et al.,\n2017)."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Across experi-\nments, we could again replicate the finding by Langer and Roth (1975) in an online ex-\nperimental setting (see also our replication study). Thus, introducing a start response\ndid not alter the illusion-of-control effect. Moreover, we replicated the finding that par-\nticipants speed up after losses compared to wins (Eben et al., 2020; Verbruggen et al.,\n2017)."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Across both experiments the authors explicitly replicate speeding after losses relative to wins.""}"
10.17705/1atrr.00026,e1d66d7f28d3d3d7,0,,,,2006,abstract,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": """", ""year"": ""2006"", ""title"": """", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""This study is an exact replication of an experiment investigating the impacts of website design on users’ ability to use a website and their opinion of the website (Galletta et al., 2006)."", ""outcome"": ""mixed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""The replication confirms the importance of website design features, specifically delay, breadth, and familiarity on users’ ability to use and form an opinion of a website. However, the replication did not confirm the main finding, that it is the interplay of these three variables, i.e., cognitive cost, that predict performance and attitude."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""abstract"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Explicit agreement and disagreement with the target findings; main interaction finding unconfirmed, but the title does not focus specifically on that interaction.""}"
10.1098/rsos.250508,e7746157bd29dd4b,1,10.1080/02699930500172291,The debt of gratitude: dissociating gratitude and indebtedness.,"Watkins P, Scheer J, Ovnicek M, Kolts R.",2006,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Watkins P, Scheer J, Ovnicek M, Kolts R."", ""year"": ""2006"", ""title"": ""The debt of gratitude: dissociating gratitude and indebtedness."", ""doi"": ""10.1080/02699930500172291"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""3"", ""evidence_quote"": ""We built our extension on the findings by Watkins et al. [6], who, like Tsang [1], argued that gratitude and indebtedness are distinct, but went further to argue that expectations for reciprocity would increase indebtedness but decrease gratitude. Tying these findings together with the experimental paradigm of Tsang [1], we aimed to examine the associations between perceived expectations for reciprocity and the inclination to reciprocate."", ""outcome"": ""mixed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Overall, we found empirical support for the hypothesis that gratitude and indebtedness are correlated to expectation for reciprocity, yet failed to find support for the association between expectation for reciprocity and reciprocity inclination.Our findings support the argument by Watkins et al. [6] that expectation for reciprocity would be associated with higher indebtedness but lower gratitude."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": false, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Extensions retest reciprocity expectations: gratitude and indebtedness comparisons agree, while the expected expectations–inclination association is unsupported. No overall verdict on all Watkins comparisons overrides this combination.""}"
10.1098/rsos.250508,e7746157bd29dd4b,2,10.1111/j.1467-9280.2006.01705.x,Gratitude and prosocial behavior: helping when it costs you.,"Bartlett MY, DeSteno D.",2006,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Bartlett MY, DeSteno D."", ""year"": ""2006"", ""title"": ""Gratitude and prosocial behavior: helping when it costs you."", ""doi"": ""10.1111/j.1467-9280.2006.01705.x"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""3"", ""evidence_quote"": ""Findings in the literature about the associations between gratitude and reciprocity have so far been mixed. For example, a seminal study by Bartlett & DeSteno [2] illustrated that gratitude is positively associated with reciprocity, whereas Peng et al. [16] failed to replicate Bartlett & DeSteno [2] and did not find any support for links with reciprocity for both gratitude and indebtedness. Therefore, our extension could be thought of as a conceptual replication of the Bartlett & DeSteno [2] and Peng et al. [16] directions to try and determine whether reciprocity might play a role, using an empirical design from a different study."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""By contrast, the absence of an association between expectations for reciprocity and reciprocity inclination in our results may be seen as lending support for the findings by Peng et al. [16] over that of Bartlett & DeSteno [2]."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": false, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Authors frame their conceptual comparison as supporting Peng over Bartlett and DeSteno. An exploratory positive gratitude–inclination correlation also appears; confidence is reduced because the discussion comparison concerns expectations rather than gratitude.""}"
10.1098/rsos.250508,e7746157bd29dd4b,3,10.1080/15534510.2019.1710248,"Gratitude, indebtedness, and reciprocity: an extended replication of Bartlett & DeSteno (2006).","Peng C, Malafosse C, Nelissen RMA, Zeelenberg M.",2020,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Peng C, Malafosse C, Nelissen RMA, Zeelenberg M."", ""year"": ""2020"", ""title"": ""Gratitude, indebtedness, and reciprocity: an extended replication of Bartlett & DeSteno (2006)."", ""doi"": ""10.1080/15534510.2019.1710248"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""3"", ""evidence_quote"": ""Findings in the literature about the associations between gratitude and reciprocity have so far been mixed. For example, a seminal study by Bartlett & DeSteno [2] illustrated that gratitude is positively associated with reciprocity, whereas Peng et al. [16] failed to replicate Bartlett & DeSteno [2] and did not find any support for links with reciprocity for both gratitude and indebtedness. Therefore, our extension could be thought of as a conceptual replication of the Bartlett & DeSteno [2] and Peng et al. [16] directions to try and determine whether reciprocity might play a role, using an empirical design from a different study."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""By contrast, the absence of an association between expectations for reciprocity and reciprocity inclination in our results may be seen as lending support for the findings by Peng et al. [16] over that of Bartlett & DeSteno [2]."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": false, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Peng 2020 is itself explicitly retested, separately from Bartlett 2006. The discussion supports Peng; exploratory gratitude and indebtedness correlations differ from the introductory description, creating uncertainty.""}"
10.3765/exabs.v0i0.603,ec68785003c85bd3,0,,"The meaning of most: Semantics, numerosity and psychology.","Pietroski, P., J. Lidz, T. Hunter & J. Halberda.",2008,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Pietroski, P., J. Lidz, T. Hunter & J. Halberda."", ""year"": ""2008"", ""title"": ""The meaning of most: Semantics, numerosity and psychology."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""My results confirm and extend the findings of Pietroski et al. (2008), Hackl (2009), Lidz et al. (2011) and indicate that semantics provides inviolable instructions to visual cognition processes."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""My results confirm and extend the findings of Pietroski et al. (2008), Hackl (2009), Lidz et al. (2011) and indicate that semantics provides inviolable instructions to visual cognition processes."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The author explicitly places this original within the set of findings confirmed and extended.""}"
10.3765/exabs.v0i0.603,ec68785003c85bd3,1,,On the grammar and processing of proportional quantifiers: most versus more than half.,"Hackl, M.",2009,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Hackl, M."", ""year"": ""2009"", ""title"": ""On the grammar and processing of proportional quantifiers: most versus more than half."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""My results confirm and extend the findings of Pietroski et al. (2008), Hackl (2009), Lidz et al. (2011) and indicate that semantics provides inviolable instructions to visual cognition processes."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""My results confirm and extend the findings of Pietroski et al. (2008), Hackl (2009), Lidz et al. (2011) and indicate that semantics provides inviolable instructions to visual cognition processes."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The author explicitly places this original within the set of findings confirmed and extended.""}"
10.1177/019874290503100104,88e177b9a1be6cb9,0,,,"Walker, Stiller, Severson, & Golly",1998,abstract,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Walker, Stiller, Severson, & Golly"", ""year"": ""1998"", ""title"": """", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""This article describes a replication of the First Step to Success program (Walker, Stiller, Severson, & Golly, 1998) with at-risk students in the first and second grade to determine program effectiveness in decreasing inappropriate behaviors and increasing academic engagement time."", ""outcome"": ""cannot_be_determined"", ""outcome_quote"": """", ""outcome_quote_section"": """", ""outcome_confident"": false, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The abstract reports improved behavior but gives neither the original finding nor an overall replication verdict. Its citation identifies a program source, whose full reference and relationship to the original finding cannot be resolved from this abstract.""}"
10.2307/2061038,56291071a4afe166,0,,,Jones; Westoff,1979,abstract,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Jones; Westoff"", ""year"": ""1979"", ""title"": """", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Jones and Westoff (1979) reported a study of fertility trends among white women in intact first marriages."", ""outcome"": ""mixed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""We found that the fertility of the two religious groups was indeed converging, but the Catholic-non-Catholic differential was larger than that found by Jones and Westoff --how much larger depends on what measure is used. Moreover, we found that the differential by frequency of communion among Catholic wives was not converging."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""abstract"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The abstract reports agreement on fertility convergence between religious groups but disagreement on convergence by communion frequency.""}"
10.1167/12.11.12,edb96f4b87f5413a,0,10.1167/8.7.2,Perceptual grouping and inverse fMRI activity patterns in human visual cortex.,"Fang, F., Kersten, D., & Murray, S. O.",2008,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Fang, F., Kersten, D., & Murray, S. O."", ""year"": ""2008"", ""title"": ""Perceptual grouping and inverse fMRI activity patterns in human visual cortex."", ""doi"": ""10.1167/8.7.2"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""It is perhaps not surprising that our study replicates the\nreduction found in Murray and colleagues’ previous\nwork, given that we explicitly set out to replicate the\nparameters used in the study of Fang et al."", ""outcome"": ""mixed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""This model\nhas previously been motivated based on data showing\nthat organized (and thus predictable) percepts lead to\nreduced activation levels on the primary visual cortex\n(Fang et al., 2008; Murray et al., 2002). The results of\nthe current study replicate this reduction, but ﬁnd that\nthis reduction is not speciﬁc to the sensory representation of this stimulus; rather, it also occurs in retinotopic\nregions that do not respond to the sensory onset of the\nstimulus. Importantly however this reduction is not\nfound in all visual areas (which might suggest a general\narousal effect); rather, our results also replicate an\nincrease in activation for the organized percept in the\nhigher-level shape selective region LO (Fang et al.,\n2008)."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The V1 decrease and LO increase replicate, but the paper expressly contrasts its retinotopically nonspecific decrease with Fang et al.’s claim of retinotopic specificity. Confirming the decrease alone is an effect-specific comparison, not a verdict on the replication as a whole. Indeed this assumption was made explicit in Fang et al. (2008), who stated that ‘‘we are conﬁdent that the modulations in the fMRI signal that we observed occurred in the retinotopic representation of the stimulus and not in immediately adjacent retinotopic regions’’ (p. 6). In contrast, when we investigated this directly it became evident that, although there was a clear reduction in activation at the retinotopic location of the stimuli, this reduction did not appear to be speciﬁc to, or even selectively stronger in, the retinotopic location of the stimulus. ""}"
10.1177/27550311231225957,0306af354d9b6f4b,0,,,,2012,abstract,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": """", ""year"": ""2012"", ""title"": """", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": """", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""In this study, we conduct a quasi-replication of Zavyalova and colleagues’ (2012) influential product harm paper, which explored how firm impression management strategies shaped media tenor following misconduct."", ""outcome"": ""cannot_be_determined"", ""outcome_quote"": """", ""outcome_quote_section"": """", ""outcome_confident"": false, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Abstract states dissimilar empirical results but does not identify which comparisons agree or disagree. Full text could not be acquired; original reference entry not available in saved text.""}"
10.1167/jov.20.6.18,84cdf9f12aedb720,0,10.1167/6.10.4,Kin recognition and the perceived facial similarity of children,"Maloney, L. T., & Dal Martello, M. F.",2006,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Maloney, L. T., & Dal Martello, M. F."", ""year"": ""2006"", ""title"": ""Kin recognition and the perceived facial similarity of children"", ""doi"": ""10.1167/6.10.4"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Facial similarity between individuals informs kinship judgments in third-party kin recognition. Indeed, one study found that similarity and kinship judgments encapsulate the same information (Maloney & Dal Martello, 2006). Yet, another study found that this is not the case when comparing adult face pairs of different sex (DeBruine et al., 2009). We replicated these studies to further clarify the role of facial similarity in kin recognition."", ""outcome"": ""mixed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""In conclusion, using a larger stimuli sample of children, we replicated Maloney and Dal Martello's (\n2006\n) finding that kinship and similarity ratings are highly correlated. We found that the effect of sex was the same on similarity and on kinship judgments, contrary to\nDeBruine et al (2009)\n. However, we found that the effect of age was different on similarity judgments than on kinship judgments, contrary to\nMaloney and Dal Martello (2006)\n."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": """"}"
10.1093/jos/ffaa001,82f2258ef770fc93,0,,,,2004,abstract,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": """", ""year"": ""2004"", ""title"": """", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": """", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""1, 2"", ""evidence_quote"": ""Abstract This paper revisits a study by Machery et al. (2004), suggesting that, in experimental versions of Kripke’s (1980) fictional cases on the use of proper names, Westerners are more likely than East Asian participants to show intuitions compatible with Kripke’s causal-historical (CH) theory of reference."", ""outcome"": ""mixed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""We replicated the results of Machery et al. (2004), but we show that the residual finding—i.e., that participants who are not aligned with CH produce responses consistent with a definite descriptions (DD) theory of reference—does not hold."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""abstract"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Abstract explicitly reports replication of results and failure of the residual finding. No reference list available to copy target citation.""}"
10.1111/j.1471-6402.1997.tb00120.x,72beb18f4e8c5a25,1,,Overworked and underpaid.,"Major, B., McFarlin, D., Gagnon, D.",1984,abstract,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Major, B., McFarlin, D., Gagnon, D."", ""year"": ""1984"", ""title"": ""Overworked and underpaid."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Previous research has suggested that women pay themselves significantly less than men pay themselves for the same amount of work (Callahan-Levy & Messé, 1979; Major, McFarlin, & Gagnon, 1984). In an experimental study involving 132 participants (68 men and 64 women), this “depressed-entitlement effect” was replicated in a current sample of university students."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Previous research has suggested that women pay themselves significantly less than men pay themselves for the same amount of work (Callahan-Levy & Messé, 1979; Major, McFarlin, & Gagnon, 1984). In an experimental study involving 132 participants (68 men and 64 women), this “depressed-entitlement effect” was replicated in a current sample of university students."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""abstract"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The abstract attributes the effect to both studies and states that this effect was replicated in the new sample.""}"
10.17705/1atrr.00032,d29f41a193cc6e12,1,,Profiling the research productivity of tenured Information Systems faculty at U.S. institutions.,"Dean, D. L., Lowry, P. B., & Humpherys, S.",2011,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Dean, D. L., Lowry, P. B., & Humpherys, S."", ""year"": ""2011"", ""title"": ""Profiling the research productivity of tenured Information Systems faculty at U.S. institutions."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Following their procedures, we limited our sample to recent cases of designated IS scholars in U.S. business schools, but used a more recent time frame, and found very similar results. Details of our modest replication of their study are found in the Appendix (Table A1)."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Our results are very close to those in Dean et al. (2011). They found that promoted IS scholars published 23% of their articles in CS and Engineering journals; we find 24%. They found 22% of articles in non-IS business journals; we find 21%."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""fulltext"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The appendix explicitly reports a separate replication in a new sample of promoted faculty and compares the original publication proportions with almost identical new proportions.""}"
10.1002/jaba.504,ea4f23db387142d5,1,10.1007/s10803-015-2617-0,The generality of interview-informed functional analyses: Systematic replications in school and home.,"Santiago, J. L., Hanley, G. P., Moore, K., & Jin, C. S.",2016,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Santiago, J. L., Hanley, G. P., Moore, K., & Jin, C. S."", ""year"": ""2016"", ""title"": ""The generality of interview-informed functional analyses: Systematic replications in school and home."", ""doi"": ""10.1007/s10803-015-2617-0"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""We replicated and extended Hanley et al. (2014) and Santiago et al. (2016)."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""We replicated and extended Hanley et al. (2014) and Santiago et al. (2016). We demonstrated the positive effects of stregthening FCRs, tolerance responses, and engagement with reinforcers identiﬁed via interview and analysis on problem behaviors with two young children diagnosed with ASD."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The authors explicitly state that they replicated and extended both named studies, and describe positive treatment effects. This overall verdict covers both targets.""}"
10.1002/jaba.613,b99fe2c5d5675e4b,1,10.1002/jaba.67,Effects of two variations of differential reinforcement on prompt dependency,"Cividini-Motta, C., & Ahearn, W. H.",2013,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Cividini-Motta, C., & Ahearn, W. H."", ""year"": ""2013"", ""title"": ""Effects of two variations of differential reinforcement on prompt dependency"", ""doi"": ""10.1002/jaba.67"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""The current research sought to systematically replicate Smaby et al. (2007) and Cividini-Motta and Ahearn’s (2013) use of the MSA, speci ﬁ cally to evaluate the relative reinforcing ef ﬁ cacy of the individual components of a token economy in a brief assessment."", ""outcome"": ""descriptive only"", ""outcome_quote"": ""The current research sought to systematically replicate Smaby et al. (2007) and Cividini-Motta and Ahearn’s (2013) use of the MSA, speci ﬁ cally to evaluate the relative reinforcing ef ﬁ cacy of the individual components of a token economy in a brief assessment."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""introduction"", ""outcome_confident"": false, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Authors explicitly replicate use of the assessment in a token-economy application. Discussion confirms utility of the method but does not compare the specific earlier social-reinforcer or prompt-dependency effects. Methodological success alone is not a finding comparison.""}"
10.31234/osf.io/j2xn3,43583d8e5bbd8f00,0,10.31234/osf.io/x6ehu,The Everyday Belief Bias: Belief in the Claim of a Political Argument Predicts the Perceived Quality of the Argument.,"Deans-Browne, C., & Singmann, H.",2024,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Deans-Browne, C., & Singmann, H."", ""year"": ""2024"", ""title"": ""The Everyday Belief Bias: Belief in the Claim of a Political Argument Predicts the Perceived Quality of the Argument."", ""doi"": ""10.31234/osf.io/x6ehu"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""We also replicate the belief consistency effect found previously in the Everyday Belief Bias Task (Deans-Browne & Singmann, 2024); participants think arguments are of better quality if the arguments are also in line with their beliefs."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""We also replicate the belief consistency effect found previously in the Everyday Belief Bias Task (Deans-Browne & Singmann, 2024); participants think arguments are of better quality if the arguments are also in line with their beliefs. We also replicate that the maximum belief bias effect was larger than the largest effect of argument type (i.e., the average difference in argument quality ratings between good and authority-based arguments). Furthermore, we replicated the failure to find a linear or quadratic interaction between argument type and belief consistency, suggesting that the effect of argument type is somewhat similar irrespective of how in line the argument is with an individual’s belief."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The authors explicitly confirm belief consistency, its greater magnitude than argument type, and the original absence of the interaction. The new comparison between types of bad argument is an extension.""}"
10.1111/2041-210x.13602,a6ce639055c5e466,1,10.7554/eLife.46923,Consistent and correctable bias in metagenomic sequencing experiments.,"McLaren, M. R., Willis, A. D., & Callahan, B. J.",2019,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""McLaren, M. R., Willis, A. D., & Callahan, B. J."", ""year"": ""2019"", ""title"": ""Consistent and correctable bias in metagenomic sequencing experiments."", ""doi"": ""10.7554/eLife.46923"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""We recovered Elbrecht et al.'s (2017)\nfinding that efficiency is higher when species evenness is higher\n(Figure 2; Figure S1), and we found that OTU sizes are a poor pre-\ndictor of input genomic DNA, which confirms the conventional wis-\ndom that OTU size is a poor predictor of species relative abundances\n(Figure S2; McLaren et al., 2019)."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""We recovered Elbrecht et al.'s (2017)\nfinding that efficiency is higher when species evenness is higher\n(Figure 2; Figure S1), and we found that OTU sizes are a poor pre-\ndictor of input genomic DNA, which confirms the conventional wis-\ndom that OTU size is a poor predictor of species relative abundances\n(Figure S2; McLaren et al., 2019)."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_confident"": true}"
10.1111/2041-210x.13602,a6ce639055c5e466,2,10.1128/Aem.05220-11,Barcoded primers used in multiplex amplicon pyrosequencing bias amplification.,"Berry, D., Ben Mahfoudh, K., Wagner, M., & Loy, A.",2011,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Berry, D., Ben Mahfoudh, K., Wagner, M., & Loy, A."", ""year"": ""2011"", ""title"": ""Barcoded primers used in multiplex amplicon pyrosequencing bias amplification."", ""doi"": ""10.1128/Aem.05220-11"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""We also find no evidence for ‘tag bias’, which is the hypoth-\nesis that the sample-­\nidentifying nucleotide sequences attached to\nPCR primers might promote annealing to some template-­\nDNA se-\nquences over others, exacerbating taxonomic bias in PCR (e.g. Berry\net al., 2011; O'Donnell et al., 2016)."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""We found no evidence for tag bias in PCR amplification. For instance,\nunder optimal PCR conditions (A & B), pairs using the same tags (A1/\nB1, A2/B2, A3/B3) and pairs using different tags (e.g. A1/B2, A2/\nB1, A3/B2, …) both generated almost identical NMDS ordinations\n(Figure 3). Under non-­\noptimal PCRs, we still found no evidence for\ntag bias, even though at higher annealing temperatures, some tag\nsequences might be more likely to aid primer annealing (Figures S3\nand S4). Note that we did not correct the p-­\nvalues for three tests,\nunderlining the lack of evidence for tag bias."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""results"", ""outcome_confident"": true}"
10.1111/2041-210x.13602,a6ce639055c5e466,3,10.1371/journal.pone.0148698,Indexed PCR primers induce template-specific bias in large-scale DNA sequencing studies.,"O'Donnell, J. L., Kelly, R. P., Lowell, N. C., & Port, J. A.",2016,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""O'Donnell, J. L., Kelly, R. P., Lowell, N. C., & Port, J. A."", ""year"": ""2016"", ""title"": ""Indexed PCR primers induce template-specific bias in large-scale DNA sequencing studies."", ""doi"": ""10.1371/journal.pone.0148698"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""We also find no evidence for ‘tag bias’, which is the hypoth-\nesis that the sample-­\nidentifying nucleotide sequences attached to\nPCR primers might promote annealing to some template-­\nDNA se-\nquences over others, exacerbating taxonomic bias in PCR (e.g. Berry\net al., 2011; O'Donnell et al., 2016)."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""We found no evidence for tag bias in PCR amplification. For instance,\nunder optimal PCR conditions (A & B), pairs using the same tags (A1/\nB1, A2/B2, A3/B3) and pairs using different tags (e.g. A1/B2, A2/\nB1, A3/B2, …) both generated almost identical NMDS ordinations\n(Figure 3). Under non-­\noptimal PCRs, we still found no evidence for\ntag bias, even though at higher annealing temperatures, some tag\nsequences might be more likely to aid primer annealing (Figures S3\nand S4). Note that we did not correct the p-­\nvalues for three tests,\nunderlining the lack of evidence for tag bias."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""results"", ""outcome_confident"": true}"
10.1080/02699206.2018.1504989,a3bfced90a859553,1,,Vocal Tract Control in Parkinson's Disease.,"Logemann, J. A. & Fisher, H. B.",1981,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Logemann, J. A. & Fisher, H. B."", ""year"": ""1981"", ""title"": ""Vocal Tract Control in Parkinson's Disease."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Influential reports on speech changes in people with Parkinson’s disease (Logemann et al, 1978, 1981) reported a posterior to anterior pattern of loss of speech sound accuracy. These claims have never been examined. In a partial replication of Logemann et al’s work we examined whether posterior lingual sounds are most affected in people with Parkinson’s disease, followed by anterior lingual sounds then labial sounds."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""This study sought to re-examine the long propagated assumption that there is a posterior- anterior progression in articulatory breakdown in PD. The current data failed to uphold the assertion."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""fulltext"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Explicit overall conclusion rejects the common posterior-to-anterior assertion in both original papers.""}"
10.31979/etd.eh36-m2wx,551ceb3696631a2f,0,,,Condry and Condry,,abstract,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Condry and Condry"", ""year"": """", ""title"": """", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""This was tested using a conceptual replication of an influential gender label study by Condry and Condry in which participants viewed a video of a baby crying in response to a startling event."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Contrary to the Condry findings, this study found that participant perception of why a baby was crying was not consistent with the gender label given (consistent, being angry for the boy label and fearful for the girl label). Participant ratings did not vary significantly based on the gender label."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""abstract"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The abstract directly contrasts the tested gender-label finding with the original; reference details are absent.""}"
10.7287/peerj.preprints.804v1,2558baa058315658,0,,,,2013,abstract,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": """", ""year"": ""2013"", ""title"": """", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": """", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""A study by White et al. found that population disease burden predicted preference for attractive politicians in U.S. congressional elections. Aim: We aimed to replicate this finding using data from the United Kingdom."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""None of the health metrics predicted rated sexiness of members of parliament."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""abstract"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The original association is explicitly described and the corresponding UK analysis found none. Target bibliography unavailable; do not import it from the later published version.""}"
10.1167/15.14.1,f2188a45e3b6d8c4,0,10.1016/j.visres.2009.01.006,Low-level features determine brightness in White’s and Benary’s illusions.,"Salmela, V. R., & Laurinen, P. I.",2009,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Salmela, V. R., & Laurinen, P. I."", ""year"": ""2009"", ""title"": ""Low-level features determine brightness in White’s and Benary’s illusions."", ""doi"": ""10.1016/j.visres.2009.01.006"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""We replicated and extended Salmela and Laurinen’s (2009) results, and\n     tested a number of diferent multi-scale spatial iltering models with respect to\n     their capability to reproduce the psychophysically observed efects of narrow-\n     band noise on lightness perception."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Still, despite these diiculties with interpreting precisely what the\n     lightness matches mean, the data consistently demonstrate that for most ob-\n     servers, noise in the range from 1 to 5 cpd has the largest efect on perceived\n     lightness, and that the most efective noise frequency does not scale propor-\n     tionally to the grating frequency."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The stated original noise-masking finding is observed again: strongest masking from 1 to 5 cpd. Tests of theoretical models with new stimuli are not reanalyses of original data.""}"
10.17605/osf.io/8wd2b,c20f9db39f03cdd4,0,,Do defaults save lives?,"Johnson, E. J., & Goldstein, D.",2003,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Johnson, E. J., & Goldstein, D."", ""year"": ""2003"", ""title"": ""Do defaults save lives?"", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": ""Experiment 1"", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""In two well-powered samples (N = 1920), we conducted a close replication of the default effect in Johnson and Goldstein (2003) and in Johnson, Bellman, and Lohse (2002)."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""We successfully replicated Johnson and Goldstein (2003)."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""abstract"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Overall verdict confirms this target despite smaller effects; both samples contributed to Part 1.""}"
10.17605/osf.io/8wd2b,c20f9db39f03cdd4,2,,,Van Dalen and Henkens,2014,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Van Dalen and Henkens"", ""year"": ""2014"", ""title"": """", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""We based our extension on Van Dalen and Henkens (2014) who found that organ donation rates were higher when the option was temporary and would have to be renewed than when the default option was to donate."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""In the first extension, we predicted that people would be more inclined to become donors when consent to organ donation is temporary. We found no evidence that consent varied between the temporary and permanent conditions."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""results"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Extension 1 explicitly retests this cited temporal-choice finding in Sample 1. The supplied cache lacks its reference entry; title and DOI cannot be copied.""}"
10.1891/jcop.2005.19.4.309,63030915d136ccf9,0,,,,,abstract,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": """", ""year"": """", ""title"": """", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": """", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Research to date suggests that the best treatment for many mental disorders is provided through the combination of medications and psychotherapy (e.g., Hollon, Thase, & Markowitz, 2002). However, the majority of studies supporting this point have been done utilizing highly restricted, controlled samples that may not accurately reflect the way mental illness is treated in the community. The present study attempted to replicate those findings in an outpatient community clinic."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Results showed that clients who took medications fared no better than clients who did not. These preliminary findings suggest that the combination of medications and psychotherapy may not be as effective in the “real world” as prior studies have implied, and point to the need for further research utilizing community samples."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""abstract"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The original claim of superior outcomes with combined medication and psychotherapy was explicitly retested. Authors report no advantage for combined treatment and contrast that result with prior findings.""}"
10.31275/20211931,ba34c6d310f46998,0,10.1063/1.2388760,Self-organization of temporal structures—A possible solution for the intervention problem.,"von Lucadou, W.",2006,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""von Lucadou, W."", ""year"": ""2006"", ""title"": ""Self-organization of temporal structures—A possible solution for the intervention problem."", ""doi"": ""10.1063/1.2388760"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""1"", ""evidence_quote"": ""In the study reported here, the choice was made for a ‘button\u0002push’ scheme, as in the studies by von Lucadou (2006) and Walach\net al. (2020), but to only use psychological variables obtained before\nphysical ones for the main analysis."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""1) The conceptual replication of the CMM-type experiment of\nthe type performed before (von Lucadou, 2006; Walach et al., 2020)\n(Experiment 1 as reported above) has yielded no evidence of anoma\u0002lous correlations."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true}"
