10.31577/sp.2021.03.824,d5d12db97a6155d5,1,10.1037/0022-3514.87.1.38,Stereotype threat and performance: How self-stereotypes influence processing by inducing regulatory foci.,"Seibt, B., & Forster, J.",2004,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Seibt, B., & Forster, J."", ""year"": ""2004"", ""title"": ""Stereotype threat and performance: How self-stereotypes influence processing by inducing regulatory foci."", ""doi"": ""10.1037/0022-3514.87.1.38"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Following Seibt and Forster (2004), we also considered analytical reasoning as a mediator of the effect of stereotype threat on risk preferences."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""The authors claimed that activation of negative stereotypes should make threatened individuals more cautious and accurate in analytical tasks. However, this was not true for our study. Activation of negative stereotypes showed to have no effect on the threatened individuals’ ability to reason analytically and, generally, analytical reasoning was unrelated to risk preferences as measured by any of the three instruments."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The authors directly compare the tested finding with this cited original.""}"
